{"step_index":293,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T15:07:30Z","thinking":"Desktop OS keyrings encrypt cookie data, with variations across operating systems, including v10/v11 formats and specific keychain services. Linux systems, however, may employ either a hardcoded key or GNOME Keyring/Secret Service, which may be identified by the \"v10\" or \"v11\" prefix. Investigating Linux cookie encryption reveals AES-128-CBC usage, with a key derived from either Secret Service/GNOME Keyring.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c '\\nimport sqlite3, shutil, os\\nfrom Crypto.Cipher import AES\\nimport hashlib\\n\\ndb_path = \\\"/home/koby/.config/BraveSoftware/Brave-Browser/Default/Cookies\\\"\\nshutil.copy2(db_path, \\\"/tmp/cookies_copy.sqlite\\\")\\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\\\"/tmp/cookies_copy.sqlite\\\")\\nc = conn.cursor()\\nc.execute(\\\"SELECT name, value, encrypted_value FROM cookies WHERE host_key LIKE \\\\\\\"%wikitech%\\\\\\\"\\\")\\nrows = c.fetchall()\\nfor name, val, enc in rows:\\n    print(\\\"NAME:\\\", name)\\n    print(\\\"  RAW VAL:\\\", val)\\n    if enc:\\n        print(\\\"  ENC PREFIX:\\\", enc[:3])\\nconn.close()\\nos.remove(\\\"/tmp/cookies_copy.sqlite\\\")\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/home/koby\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Inspecting cookie prefix\"","toolSummary":"\"Check encrypted cookie prefix\""}}]}
