{"step_index":355,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T08:26:47Z","thinking":"Planning a management-focused document requires prioritizing clarity and technical guarantees without excessive jargon. The executive summary needs to concisely state the objective: replacing the legacy, fragmented email infrastructure with a unified, modern platform. This requires clear articulation of benefits for non-technical audiences.\n\n\nThe current email infrastructure faces significant challenges due to its decentralized, outdated Qmail setup across multiple regions, leading to complex maintenance and heightened operational risks. Consolidating into a unified email solution is necessary to reduce the technical debt and streamline management. This unification will enhance security, reduce costs, and improve the user experience with a modern webmail interface.\n\n\nThe core components of the proposed architecture include a robust mail transfer agent, a secure mailbox manager, and a user-friendly webmail interface.  A dedicated security gateway provides comprehensive filtering against spam, viruses, and malware, while central authentication is managed via existing directory services.  Security is designed into each layer through measures such as end-to-end encryption, and protection against brute-force attacks.\n\n\nFocusing on security details, the architecture mandates end-to-end encryption for all user connections; no unencrypted traffic is permitted.  The system employs strict password protection by encrypting all credentials in the directory, never storing them in plain text, while maintaining existing user password hashes to minimize disruption. Access to the directory is governed by a least-privilege service account to prevent unauthorized modifications.\n\n\nThe architecture includes a dedicated security gateway for spam and phishing protection, acting as a double security barrier for all inbound and outbound emails, and providing ransomware and spoofing protection via signature verification and malware analysis. A rollback plan is in place to ensure instant recovery, utilizing a single source of truth for the\n<truncated 372 bytes>\n web server within a DMZ.\n\n\nThe system architecture details the data flow between user access, a secure frontend, the mail server, the security gateway, and the directory service.  HTTPS secures user access to the frontend. Internally, the frontend directs traffic to the mail server, which then authenticates users against the directory. Mail server interactions with the security gateway handle inbound and outbound email.\n\n\nThe diagram shows mail flow originating from users through webmail and clients, routing through the mail transfer agent (MTA), the security gateway, and finally to secure storage.  The security gateway filters all mail traffic, particularly on port 25, to implement spam and malware protection before final delivery. The network matrix confirms secure access through HTTPS for webmail and IMAPS for clients, reinforcing the end-to-end encryption strategy.\n\n\nThe table details the key network services and their associated ports for the email infrastructure, ensuring secure email submission, filtering, internal delivery, and authentication.  Project organization outlines roles for project management, security, and operational support.\n\nThe deployment plan outlines a staged approach, beginning with platform setup and security validation before progressing to a pilot domain and finally a full system migration. The migration will be conducted progressively to minimize downtime during the final switchover from the legacy infrastructure.\n\n\nThe migration strategy will proceed in four phases: a pilot program to validate user experience, automated conversion of legacy email archives, a staged domain-by-domain rollout across different regions, and finally decommissioning of the outdated servers. Success hinges on uninterrupted service for users, avoiding any email loss through continuous mailbox synchronization, and a transparent transition with no password changes. The project also aims to improve the environment by removing outdated servers, mitigating potential security flaws, and reducing hosting costs.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/update_lld_management.py\\nimport json, subprocess\\n\\ndoc_id = \\\"e76fbbe9-9f0f-4491-879c-cf68c25b139a\\\"\\ntitle = \\\"LLD - Migration Qmail vers Postfix/Dovecot\\\"\\n\\ndoc_text = \\\"\\\"\\\"## Synthèse Managériale (Executive Summary)\\n\\n* **Objectif stratégique** : Remplacer l'infrastructure historique de messagerie (Qmail / déployée vers 2012-2014) dispersée sur trois territoires (Martinique, Guadeloupe, Guyane) par une plateforme unifiée, moderne et haute performance (**Postfix + Dovecot + Roundcube**) hébergée sur `mtq-mailsrvp-01`.\\n* **Les 4 gains majeurs pour l'entreprise** :\\n  1. **Sécurité & Conformité renforcées** : Chiffrement systématique de tous les accès (TLS 1.3), protection périmétrique anti-phishing/antispam avec **Proxmox Mail Gateway (PMG)**, et fermeture de serveurs obsolètes non patchables.\\n  2. **Réduction des coûts & de la dette technique** : Remplacement de multiples machines hétérogènes par un serveur consolidé, réduisant l'empreinte infrastructure et les coûts de maintenance.\\n  3. **Expérience utilisateur modernisée** : Nouvelle interface Webmail Roundcube plus ergonomique, rapide et responsive sur smartphones.\\n  4. **Risque opérationnel maîtrisé (Zéro rupture)** : Démarche par étapes démarrant avec un domaine pilote propre (`ool.fr` — 12 comptes), sans changement de mot de passe pour les utilisateurs et avec possibilité de retour arrière instantané.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n### 1. Pourquoi ce projet ? (Constat actuel)\\nL'infrastructure actuelle repose sur une stack **Qmail-LDAP** historique présentant plusieurs freins majeurs :\\n* **Dispersion géographique et silos** : Trois sites distincts (Martinique `smtp-mtq-1`, Guadeloupe `smtp-gua-1`, Guyane `smtp-guy-1`) cumulant plus de 300 000 comptes, sans synchronisation native standard.\\n* **Obsolescence logicielle** : Des briques logicielles figées depuis plus de 10 ans, avec des formats de stockage propriétaires (ex: anciens dossiers webmail `sentmail-*` de 2012-2014) bloquant l'\n<truncated 12672 bytes>","Cwd":"\"/home/koby\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Updating LLD document\"","toolSummary":"\"Update LLD with simplified executive view & security section\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking","tool_calls"]}
